// A `Login` response that carries no scope logs alice as its caller. Under the strict mirror rule,
// a caller field is logged only by an event that carries the caller, so ingestion rejects it
// (`Rejection.nonconforming`), and alice's `Read` has no login to rely on: the specification
// denies it. The crate does the same under its default rule, `MirrorRule::Strict`: it rejects the
// response and denies the `Read`. Under the opt-out `MirrorRule::Current`, which accepts any
// logged caller when the request side does not resolve, it would ingest the response and allow the
// `Read`.
@id("read_after_login")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"Read", resource)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::response{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
